{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011ChTBKsCJZf6TQQitpuVdG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Yes.** One suspicious package was installed through apt:\n\n**`xmrig` (version 6.21.0-1)** — installed on **2026-04-10**.\n\nXMRig is a well-known open-source Monero cryptocurrency miner. Its presence on a host — especially installed via `apt` — is a strong indicator of compromise, as it is commonly deployed by attackers after gaining access to hijack compute resources for cryptomining.\n\n### Logs examined\n\n| Log file | What it shows |\n|---|---|\n| `fs/var/log/apt/term.log` (lines 2–4) | Records the unpacking and setup of `xmrig 6.21.0-1` on 2026-04-10 at 09:00 |\n| `fs/var/log/apt/history.log` (lines 1–4) | Records only a `curl` install on 2026-04-12 — notably, **xmrig does not appear here**, suggesting the history log may have been rotated or tampered with to hide the install |\n\nThe discrepancy between the two logs is itself a finding: `term.log` captured the dpkg-level terminal output of the xmrig installation, but `history.log` (which records apt commands and their metadata) contains no corresponding entry for it. The only entry in `history.log` is the later `curl` install. This gap could indicate the attacker cleared or rotated `history.log` after installing xmrig, though the compressed `history.log.2.gz` (134 bytes uncompressed) likely holds the original rotated entry."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":170,"cache_read_input_tokens":23280,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":170},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"abc90b70-c1e8-4aa6-bc44-3ce36360c7b0","uuid":"322dc428-22eb-4581-9f92-3a56d6ff